# ── Local only. These are in the working tree and must never be pushed. ──────
# Captures of Innovue's IPtech platform rendering a client's patent data, the
# crops taken from them, and the two artifacts built out of those crops.
# CLAUDE.md explains why the line sits here and not at "anything about Innovue".
#
# All three capture folders moved under visual-reference/ on 2026-09-08. The
# parent entry covers whatever is put there next; the bare names below are kept
# because this file has already been defeated once by a rename, and a stale
# entry costs nothing while a missing one cost 6 MB.
visual-reference/
iptech-screenshots/
iptech-screenshots-identified/
design/previews/comparison-assets/   # moved under visual-reference/, kept as a hint
design/previews/iptech-terrain-comparison.html
design/previews/iptech-feature-request.html

# Mood-board captures. ALL names: the folder was visual_inspo/, was renamed to
# visual-inspiration/ outside the repo on 2026-09-08 -- at which point the old
# entry stopped matching and `git add -A` staged all 6 MB of it, caught before
# the commit -- and now lives under visual-reference/. The raster rule at the
# bottom is the real guard; these are hints.
visual_inspo/
visual-inspiration/

# A third party's trademark files. Cited in the documents, not redistributed --
# with one exception, and it is the whole folder minus one file.
#
# The product attributes Innovue, and from 2026-09-08 it does so with their mark
# rather than with the words alone: docs/brief.md section 3 carries the argument
# and the two limits on it. An attribution line that ships a broken image is not
# an attribution, so the variants the dashboard renders are tracked -- TWO of
# them, because the line is theme-aware: their primary blue in light, their own
# white mark in dark. The other six stay out -- the Chinese lockups because
# nothing here is bilingual, the other colourways because nothing uses them.
#
# The trailing /* is load-bearing and is not a tidier spelling of the directory.
# Git will not descend into an excluded DIRECTORY, so a negation under one never
# fires -- `brand/logos/innovue/` followed by a ! line silently ignores all eight
# and reports nothing. Excluding the CONTENTS leaves the directory walkable and
# the negation works. Verified with git check-ignore, which is the only way to
# see this: the wrong form fails exactly like the right one until you look.
brand/logos/innovue/*
!brand/logos/innovue/Innovue_Logo_Blue_eng_inline.svg
!brand/logos/innovue/Innovue_Logo_Light_eng_inline.svg

# ── Build output and cruft ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
.DS_Store
node_modules/
.next/
dist/
build/
*.log
.env
.env.local

# ── The invariant, not a name blocklist ──────────────────────────────────────
# This repository legitimately contains no raster images at all: the pages are
# HTML, the marks are SVG, the typefaces are woff2. Every screenshot here is
# either a capture of a third party's product or a mood board, and neither is
# ours to publish. So rasters are refused wholesale -- a directory rename
# cannot get around this the way it got around the entries above.
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.gif
*.webp
*.bmp
*.tiff

# ── The one permitted raster family. Read the rule above first. ──────────────
# Terrain's own marketing gradient for the website's Products menu, in the same
# three-file shape as licensing-warm-v2 and signal-cool: a 640x413 original as
# the <img> fallback plus the two webp widths the image build emits.
#
# It clears the rule above on the distinction that rule was actually drawing:
# these are OURS. Not a capture of a third party's product, not a mood board.
# Neither of the two reasons rasters were refused applies to them.
#
# But it IS a weakening, so it is scoped as narrowly as the syntax allows --
# three literal paths, no glob. A rename is still refused. A fourth file in the
# same directory is still refused. The invariant still catches what the
# path-based entries at the top of this file failed to.
#
# Adding a product means adding its three paths here, deliberately, one line
# each. If that ever feels tedious enough to replace with a glob, that is the
# guard working.
!brand/assets/imagery/terrain/terrain-forest.jpg
!brand/assets/imagery/terrain/terrain-forest-400.webp
!brand/assets/imagery/terrain/terrain-forest-640.webp
